  "authored_student_inputs_complete": true,
  "completed_passage": "Our group was making a model shelf for the school reading corner. I had drawn the plan and felt very confident about it. When Mina asked me to check the numbers again, I thought she was simply delaying the work. We had only one afternoon left, and I wanted to start cutting.\n\nMina did not argue. She put two thick pieces of card on the table and placed the long piece between them. It would not fit. I had measured the outside width of the model but had forgotten that the two side panels would take up some of the space.\n\nFor a moment, I tried to defend my drawing. Then I saw the model in front of me and stopped making excuses. Mina was not questioning how hard I had worked; she was checking whether the parts would go together. We adjusted the width before using any wood. That small change saved material we could not replace with our limited budget.\n\nI offered Mina the pencil and asked her to add her measurements to mine. The final plan was a shared effort, with notes in two different styles of handwriting. Before making the real shelf, we built a second model to test it. This time, every part fitted.\n\nWhen classmates praised the finished shelf, I mentioned Mina's early question instead of taking all the credit. I now try to see a careful question as a chance to improve a plan, even when the plan is my own.",
